WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.330
When it comes to PC Gaming, the monitor you
choose matters a whole lot more than most

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.330
people think.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:11.049
Sure, you can buy any basic 1080p monitor
for around $100, but a gaming monitor is built

00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:13.860
FOR gaming with gaming specific features.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.880
So for this video, we’re comparing 4 of
the top gaming monitors under $200 to see

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.820
which one you should buy for your gaming needs.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.070
And we’ll have links to these monitors in
the description.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:28.210
The first one is a 24” curved monitor from
Sceptre for $163.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:33.280
Then the 24” Zowie monitor from BenQ for
$199.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.860
Then the 24” Acer Nitro monitor for $160.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:44.180
And finally, a 27” monitor from Dell for
$180, which we’ve already covered in a previous

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.770
video for being the best selling gaming monitor
on Amazon, so check that out after this.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:51.060
Before we get into build quality and design,
let’s compare the specs.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.650
The resolution’s the same across the board
with Full HD 1080P, and for panel tech, the

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:02.680
Dell and BenQ get a TN panel, while the Sceptre
gets VA and the Acer gets IPS.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:07.440
The difference is seen in a few different
things, mainly including response time, viewing

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.560
angles, color accuracy, price and refresh
rates.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.061
For viewing angles, the Acer and it’s IPS
panel easily beats out the rest, and it’s

00:01:15.061 --> 00:01:17.670
the same thing for color accuracy as well.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:22.780
However, IPS panels are more costly to produce,
especially with high refresh rates, so that’s

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:29.200
why the Acer only refreshes at 75Hz compared
to 144Hz on the rest of these panels.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.580
The 144hz model of this IPS monitor costs
a much higher $285.

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:39.730
And IPS panels typically have slower response
times, but with Acer’s Visual Response Boost

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:46.009
mode on, it’ll hit a 1ms respone time, but
some users complained about flickering issues

00:01:46.009 --> 00:01:50.900
and monitor brightness going down with VRB
enabled, which may be an issue since it’s

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:57.170
already rated at only 250 nits of brightness,
with the Dell being rated up to 400 nits.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:02.930
With Acer’s Visual Response Boost disabled,
response time goes up to 10ms, a lot slower

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:03.930
than the rest.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:08.190
The BenQ monitor is the only monitor out of
the bunch that doesn’t support FreeSync,

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:12.520
a technology that helps get rid of screen
tearing and stuttering when FPS doesn’t

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.010
match the monitors refresh rate.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:19.720
They all have VESA mounts, but the Sceptre
has a smaller 75x75mm pattern, so be aware

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:20.720
of that.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.040
Now let’s compare the physical design of
the monitors.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.030
From the front, the Acer looks the best with
the thinnest bezels overall and the best looking

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.450
stand out of the bunch.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:33.680
The BenQ has huge bezels all around and an
enlarged area for the controls, not to mention

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.470
a massive and thick stand, so it’s not the
best choice for looks.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:41.800
From the side, the sceptre is very thin and
sleek compared to the rest, and it’s the

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.209
only one that’s curved, but it’s very
subtle so it honestly doesn’t really change

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:44.209
much.

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:47.020
The BenQ and Acer are by far, the thickest
from the side.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:51.319
Due to the Sceptre’s low profile stand,
the display sits closer to the wall than the

00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:55.569
rest of the monitors, with the Dell in second
place and the BenQ’s display sitting the

00:02:55.569 --> 00:02:59.560
furthest from the wall, so if you have a small
desk, the Sceptre wins here.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.550
As for the stands, the Sceptre’s is the
only that’s made out of a solid piece of

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:08.190
aluminum, compared to plastic stands with
aluminum plates, and the Sceptre is by far

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:13.709
the sturdiest when you wiggle the desk, with
the larger BenQ and Dell wobbling the most.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:18.879
As for adjustability, the BenQ destroys the
rest of the monitors with tilt, side to side

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:24.519
swivel, up and down height and 90 degree rotatation
adjustability for a vertical setup.

00:03:24.519 --> 00:03:28.130
The rest of the monitors literally only tilt
forward and backward.

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:32.709
The main drawback is the lack of height adjustment
since some of these monitors sit pretty low.

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:36.800
As for cable management, the Dell and Acer
have basically none at all..

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.510
The Sceptre comes with a couple of plastic
clips that you can adjust and they work very

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:45.090
well, and the BenQ has a plastic clip on the
back which works great as well.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:48.650
As for the cables that come in the box, the
Sceptre is the only one that comes with an

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:53.190
external power brick, the rest of them have
power supplies built into the monitor.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:58.370
However, the Sceptre does come with a DisplayPort
cable, and same with the BenQ monitor as well.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:03.459
The Dell comes with an HDMI 2.0 cable which
isn’t as good as having Displayport since

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:07.629
Nvidia cards require DisplayPort to support
AMD’s Freesync.

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:12.209
Now the Acer actually came with an ancient
VGA cable, which is so old that the budget

00:04:12.209 --> 00:04:19.349
RX 580 from 2017 doesn’t support it, so
you’ll have to buy an HDMI cable for 75hz

00:04:19.349 --> 00:04:22.060
since the monitor doesn’t support Displayport.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:29.610
Now onto I/O, the Acer is limited to two HDMI
1.4 ports, a VGA port, an audio in port for

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:33.860
using your monitor’s speakers when you’re
using VGA, and a headphone jack.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:39.090
The Sceptre comes with two clearly labeled
HDMI ports, a DisplayPort also capable of

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.219
144hz and a headphone jack.

00:04:41.219 --> 00:04:48.199
The Dell has HDMI and DisplayPort both capable
of 144hz, a headphone jack and two USB 2.0

00:04:48.199 --> 00:04:49.479
ports for peripherals.

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:56.230
The BenQ comes with a Displayport, a Dual-link
DVI port capable of 144hz, and an HDMI port

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:58.590
which I believe is limited to 60hz.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:02.620
It also has a headphone jack on the left side
which may be more comfortable to use, and

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:07.089
it’s the only one without built-in speakers,
which honestly doesn’t really matter.

00:05:07.089 --> 00:05:10.580
Now firing them up, let’s compare the Menu
controls and UI.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:15.080
The Dell has clearly labeled controls on the
front that are very easy to use, and the UI

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:17.169
is very simple and works perfectly.

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:20.889
The Sceptre has 3 buttons on the back which
you’ll have to take a look at to understand

00:05:20.889 --> 00:05:23.240
and it’ll take some time getting used to
before you stop accidentally hitting the wrong

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:24.240
ones.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.849
The UI is a bit cluttered but it works okay
overall.

00:05:25.849 --> 00:05:30.410
The Acer has a joystick that can be used to
change every setting and it works really well,

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:34.639
along with 3 more buttons used to quickly
change things like input and brightness.

00:05:34.639 --> 00:05:36.960
The UI is simple and looks better than Dells.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:43.229
However, when you turn on 1ms response time
VRB mode, the screen dims like crazy and Freesync

00:05:43.229 --> 00:05:46.490
actually turns off, so you can’t get both
at the same time.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:52.370
You’ll be stuck at a horrible 10ms response
time if you want FreeSync and a bright display.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:57.429
Onto the BenQ, it has an unnecessary bulge
on the right side for its unlabelled controls

00:05:57.429 --> 00:06:01.479
which don’t work very well because the UI
was a pain to use compared to the rest of

00:06:01.479 --> 00:06:02.479
the monitors.

00:06:02.479 --> 00:06:06.779
And finally, we did some gaming playing Fortnite
on each monitor to test out how each well

00:06:06.779 --> 00:06:07.860
each monitor performed.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:12.960
The Sceptre played pretty well at 144hz using
FreeSync and we didn’t notice any issues

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.089
with gameplay, except that the display was
a bit dim.

00:06:16.089 --> 00:06:20.800
The Dell also played great at 144hz, there
were no issues and I loved how it was super

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.129
bright and the colors look pretty good!

00:06:23.129 --> 00:06:28.599
The BenQ, however, seemed to play at 144hz
pretty well despite not having FreeSync, with

00:06:28.599 --> 00:06:30.680
great brightness and decent colors as well.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:34.009
Now the Acer was another story, it’s limited
to 75hz, so as you can see in this slow-mo

00:06:34.009 --> 00:06:36.940
comparison, it’s definitely not as smooth,
and since the 1ms virtual response boost mode

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:43.159
dims the brightness, adds flickering and disables
FreeSync, I turned it off and dealt with 10ms

00:06:43.159 --> 00:06:46.419
response time, so it didn’t feel that smooth
compared to the rest.

00:06:46.419 --> 00:06:50.669
Because of that and the fact that it comes
with a VGA cable, the Acer is out, leaving

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:51.699
the other three.

00:06:51.699 --> 00:06:56.610
The BenQ was in 3rd place for having the huge
bezels, being very bulky overall with bad

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:00.199
controls and the worst UI, along with the
lack of FreeSync.

00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:04.429
We did enjoy all of the adjustability and
good brightness, but it’s the most expensive

00:07:04.429 --> 00:07:06.080
at $199.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:10.780
The Sceptre just barely got into 2nd place
by being priced at $160.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:15.740
The 144hz support with either HDMI or DisplayPort
was excellent, and along with the low profile

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:21.050
design with the sturdiest stand helped outweigh
the negatives of the dimmer display and subpar

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:22.099
menu controls.

00:07:22.099 --> 00:07:23.139
And the winner is..

00:07:23.139 --> 00:07:25.300
The Dell, for quite a few reasons.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.270
You get 144hz with either HDMI or DisplayPort.

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:31.270
It’s the only one that packs 2 USB ports.

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:36.719
You get FreeSync, a low 2ms response time,
the brightest display of all of these, and

00:07:36.719 --> 00:07:40.580
the largest at 27”, with pretty slim bezels
as well.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:44.989
The controls are simple and intuitive, and
Fortnite played great at 144hz.

00:07:44.989 --> 00:07:49.619
The only downsides are the slightly less sturdy
stand and the lack of cable management.

00:07:49.619 --> 00:07:53.739
Other than that, there’s really no compromises
at a great price of $180, and that’s why

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:55.369
it’s the winner.

00:07:55.369 --> 00:07:58.940
If you want to learn more about it, check
out our video review of the Dell after this

00:07:58.940 --> 00:07:59.940
video.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:03.240
We’ve left links to the best deal on all
of these monitors in the description below,

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:06.770
so if you enjoyed this video and you’ve
made your decision, use those links below

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:09.749
to help support us and click the button above
to subscribe!

00:08:09.749 --> 00:08:12.900
This has been Vadim with Max Tech, thanks
for watching and we’ll see you in the next

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:17.979
video!

